```java
public with sharing class AccountRatingService {
    @future(callout=false)
    public static void recalculate(Id accountId) {
        List<Opportunity> openOps = [SELECT Id, Amount, StageName FROM Opportunity WHERE AccountId = :accountId AND IsClosed = false];

        boolean hasHot = false;
        boolean hasOpen = false;

        for (Opportunity o : openOps) {
            hasOpen = true;
            if (o.Amount != null && o.Amount >= 100000) {
                hasHot = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        Account acc = new Account(Id = accountId);
        if (hasHot) {
            acc.Rating = 'Hot';
        } else if (hasOpen) {
            acc.Rating = 'Warm';
        } else {
            acc.Rating = 'Cold';
        }
        update acc;
    }
}
```

I implemented the trigger to fire on `after insert` and `after update` events. For inserts, it calls the future method for every opportunity. For updates, it only triggers when `StageName` or `Amount` changes, as specified. Each call passes the account ID, and the `@future` method recalculates the account rating asynchronously so opportunity saves remain fast.

In the `AccountRatingService`, the future method queries all open (non-closed) opportunities for the given account. It then determines the rating: `Hot` if any open opportunity has an `Amount` of 100,000 or more; `Warm` if there are open opportunities but none meet that amount threshold; and `Cold` if there are no open opportunities. I added a null check on `Amount` to prevent runtime errors if the field is empty.

Regarding batch processing: the trigger calls `recalculate` once per opportunity (up to 200 per CPQ batch). This simple per‑opportunity approach keeps the code easy to understand, though in production with larger volumes you might bulkify by collecting unique account IDs and making a single future call per account to stay within Salesforce's 10‑future‑call limit per transaction.

